    div.prose
      section
        span.eyebrow The problem
        h2 Local scenes are active, but their information is fragmented.
        p Show announcements live on venue calendars, flyers, ticketing sites, and social posts. Fans miss events, bands and venues are represented inconsistently, and duplicate listings make a calendar less trustworthy.
      section
        span.eyebrow The product
        h2 Build the scene around connected data.
        p Texas Heavy treats an event as part of a larger network of bands, venues, cities, media, and community activity.
        ul
          li Browse upcoming concerts and festivals across Texas cities.
          li Connect event lineups to reusable band and venue profiles.
          li Normalize dates, locations, source links, and event identity during ingestion.
          li Search and filter without flattening useful genre or location context.
          li Extend discovery with galleries and community classifieds.
          li Give administrators tools to review, correct, and enrich imported data.
      section
        span.eyebrow The reliability problem
        h2 A useful calendar has to resist duplicates and stale data.
        p Event sources describe the same show differently. URLs change, lineup order varies, and dates can be reformatted. I built normalization and upsert behavior around canonical source identity, then added review paths for ambiguous records.
        p The goal is not maximum ingestion volume; it is a calendar people can trust enough to make plans from.
      section
        span.eyebrow Engineering ownership
        h2 Domain modeling made the frontend possible.
        p I owned the event, band, venue, city, gallery, and classified models; ingestion and deduplication; admin workflows; search and discovery pages; deployment; and visual product design. This project demonstrates the data and operational work behind an interface that should feel simple.
